('112.7.b.12.A',3,'Bees get nectar from flowers, and flowers get pollinated. What kind of interaction is this?','["an interaction with no living things", "a rock and water relationship", "a sun-and-wind relationship", "a helpful relationship between two living things"]'::jsonb,3,'Both organisms benefit.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.A',4,'A pond dries up for several months. Which organism is MOST likely to struggle to survive?','["a fish that lives only in water", "a seed in the soil", "a cactus nearby", "a desert lizard"]'::jsonb,0,'Fish depend on pond water.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.B',3,'In a food web, snakes eat mice and hawks eat snakes. What will likely happen to the mice if the snakes disappear?','["they will turn into snakes", "their number will increase", "they will stop eating", "their number will disappear"]'::jsonb,1,'Fewer predators means more prey.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.B',3,'A disease kills most of the grass in a field. What is likely to happen to the rabbits?','["their number will rise quickly", "nothing will change", "their number will decrease", "they will become producers"]'::jsonb,2,'Less food means fewer rabbits.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.B',3,'What would happen to the energy in a food web if all the producers died?','["decomposers would make sunlight", "consumers would gain energy", "nothing would change", "consumers would lose their energy source"]'::jsonb,3,'Producers supply the food energy.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.B',3,'Where does the energy in a food web start?','["the Sun", "the carnivores", "the decomposers", "the herbivores"]'::jsonb,0,'Producers capture sunlight.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.B',3,'If decomposers are removed from a forest, what happens to cycling of matter?','["more nutrients return", "nutrients are not returned to the soil", "matter is created", "energy flows backward"]'::jsonb,1,'Decomposers recycle nutrients.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.B',3,'In a food web, owls eat mice and mice eat seeds. If owls are removed, what may happen to the seeds?','["the seeds turn into mice", "more seeds because owls eat them", "fewer seeds because more mice eat them", "no change"]'::jsonb,2,'More mice would eat more seeds.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.B',4,'A drought reduces the number of insects in a food web. Which organism is MOST directly affected?','["a rock", "a mushroom", "a tree that makes its own food", "a bird that eats mainly insects"]'::jsonb,3,'Insect-eating birds lose food.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.C',2,'Which describes a healthy ecosystem?','["it has a variety of organisms and balanced resources", "it has no water", "it has no producers", "it has only one kind of organism"]'::jsonb,0,'Healthy ecosystems are balanced and varied.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.C',3,'Which human activity can help an ecosystem?','["burning forests", "planting native trees", "draining all wetlands", "dumping trash in a river"]'::jsonb,1,'Native plants support wildlife.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.C',3,'Which human activity can harm an ecosystem?','["restoring a wetland", "planting a garden", "polluting a stream", "building a bird feeder"]'::jsonb,2,'Pollution harms organisms.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.C',3,'A town builds a park with native plants and a pond. How might this benefit the ecosystem?','["it adds toxic chemicals", "it dries up water sources", "it removes all animals", "it provides food and homes for wildlife"]'::jsonb,3,'Habitat supports wildlife.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.C',3,'Cutting down a large forest for farmland can lead to what?','["loss of habitat for animals", "more homes for forest animals", "more shade", "cleaner air from fewer trees"]'::jsonb,0,'Habitat loss harms species.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.C',3,'Which action helps keep a river ecosystem healthy?','["dumping oil in the river", "keeping chemicals and trash out of the water", "draining the river", "building walls of trash"]'::jsonb,1,'Clean water supports organisms.','original'),
('112.7.b.12.C',4,'A wildlife group removes an invasive plant that crowds out native plants. How does this help the ecosystem?','["it ends the food web", "it adds more invasive species", "native plants have room to grow, supporting native animals", "it removes all plants"]'::jsonb,2,'Restoring native plants restores the food web.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.A',3,'Two birds live in the same marsh. One has a long, thin beak and the other has a short, thick beak. Why might both survive there?','["they eat the same food", "their beaks do not matter", "they never need food", "they eat different foods"]'::jsonb,3,'Different structures let them use different resources.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.A',3,'A hawk has sharp talons and an owl has soft feathers for quiet flight. How do these structures help them?','["they help each catch prey in its own way", "they make them stop eating", "they help them make seeds", "they help them become producers"]'::jsonb,0,'Structures fit the way each hunts.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.A',3,'A desert fox has large ears. How do they help it survive?','["they store water", "they release body heat and help it hear prey", "they make food from sunlight", "they cause rain"]'::jsonb,1,'Large ears cool the fox and sharpen hearing.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.A',3,'A duck has webbed feet. What is the function?','["to climb trees", "to dig for seeds", "to help it swim", "to fly higher"]'::jsonb,2,'Webbed feet push against water.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.A',3,'A woodpecker has a strong, sharp beak. What is the function?','["to crack nuts only", "to catch fish", "to filter water", "to drill into trees for insects"]'::jsonb,3,'The beak is used for drilling into wood.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.A',3,'A frog and a heron both live at a pond. The frog catches insects with a sticky tongue and the heron spears fish. Why can both live there?','["they use different structures to get different food", "they use the same structures", "they are producers", "they do not need food"]'::jsonb,0,'Different structures reduce competition.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.A',4,'Two plants grow in the same dry field. One has deep roots and the other has shallow roots that spread wide. How can both survive?','["they both live on air", "they get water from different places in the soil", "neither needs water", "they compete for water at the exact same depth"]'::jsonb,1,'Different root systems use different water sources.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.B',2,'Which is an instinctive behavior?','["a chimp learning to use a stick", "a parrot copying words", "a baby sea turtle crawling to the ocean", "a dog learning to roll over"]'::jsonb,2,'Instincts are inborn.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.B',2,'Which is a learned behavior?','["a spider spinning a web", "a bird migrating by instinct", "a baby crying", "orcas hunting in groups as they are taught by their pod"]'::jsonb,3,'Learned behaviors come from experience or teaching.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.B',3,'How do instincts help animals survive?','["they let animals act correctly without being taught", "they make animals sleep all the time", "they make animals unable to move", "they stop animals from eating"]'::jsonb,0,'Instincts are inborn survival behaviors.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.B',3,'A young bird learns where to find food by watching its parents. This is what kind of behavior?','["inherited only", "learned", "instinctive", "random"]'::jsonb,1,'Watching and practicing is learning.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.B',3,'Birds flying south for winter without being taught shows what kind of behavior?','["learned", "a game", "instinctive", "acquired by practice"]'::jsonb,2,'Migration is inborn in many birds.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.B',3,'A dog learns to open a door by pushing a lever after watching its owner. Which kind of behavior is this?','["inherited", "instinctive", "not a behavior", "learned"]'::jsonb,3,'It was learned from experience.','original'),
('112.7.b.13.B',4,'A young otter is taught by its mother to crack shellfish with a rock. How does this increase its chance of survival?','["it can get food it could not otherwise reach", "it makes shellfish stronger", "it makes the otter weaker", "it helps the otter avoid water"]'::jsonb,0,'The learned skill provides food.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Science' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
